    "badge": "Best for Walking and Running",
    "category": "Double-fold treadmill, 1-10 mph, 300 lb",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The WalkingPad X25 is for people who want one machine for desk walking and real runs. Retailers list a 1 to 10 mph speed range, a 300 lb capacity and a weight of about 119 lb, with a belt of roughly 52 by 20 inches and a design that folds to about 10 inches long for storage. It has no incline, which reviewers list as its main drawback, and motor ratings vary between listings (1.5 HP, 2 HP and 1125 W rated power). It is too tall to slide under most standing desks with its handrail up, so think of it as a compact treadmill that can do desk duty rather than a true walking pad. If you only ever walk, the Z1 or the LifeSpan is the better buy.",
    "pros": [
      "1-10 mph range handles running",
      "300 lb rating per retailers",
      "Folds to about 10 in long",
      "Roughly 52 x 20 in belt"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No incline",
      "Heavy at about 119 lb",
      "Motor rating varies by listing",
      "Bulkier than a true walking pad"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy the X25 only if you will actually run on it; for walking alone, a dedicated pad is simpler and cheaper."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Walking Pads",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-walking-pad/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best walking pad for a standing desk?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people we recommend the WalkingPad Z1, which WalkingPad lists with a 47.6 by 15.7 inch walking area, a 242 lb rating and a 1 to 4 mph range, and which folds to about 5.4 inches tall. If you plan to walk most of the workday, the LifeSpan TR1200-GlowUp's 20 by 50 inch belt and walking-focused motor are worth the higher price."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many hours a day can you use a walking pad?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the model. Some, like the WalkingPad C2, carry a recommended two-hour session limit followed by a 15 minute rest. Under-desk models built for long sessions, such as the LifeSpan, are designed to support several hours of walking a day. Check the manual for your model and break long days into blocks."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What speed should I walk at while working?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most people can type comfortably between about 1 and 2 mph and handle calls or reading up to about 3 mph. Start slow for the first few days and increase gradually. If your typing accuracy drops or you are drifting on the belt, slow down."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are walking pads loud?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They are quieter than running treadmills but not silent. You will hear the motor and the rhythm of the belt, and vibration can carry through hard floors into the room below. A dense rubber mat under the pad reduces both noise and vibration."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you run on a walking pad?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most walking pads are not designed for running; many top out between 3.5 and 4 mph and use motors sized for walking. If you want to run, choose a model that lists running speeds and a longer belt, such as the WalkingPad X25, which retailers list at 1 to 10 mph."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much weight can a walking pad hold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ratings on our picks range from about 220 lb to 350 lb. Some models list different figures on different pages, so use the lowest published number and leave headroom if you will walk for long sessions."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a walking pad worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For people who sit most of the day, a walking pad is an easy way to add low-intensity movement without changing their schedule. It is not a replacement for a running treadmill or structured exercise, but walking slowly for an hour or two during work adds up over a week."
          }
        }
