---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home & Garage",
    tags: [
      "best walking pad",
      "under desk treadmill",
      "walking pad treadmill",
      "best under desk treadmill",
      "walking treadmill for desk"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Desk-Ready Picks
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Walking Pads</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven under-desk treadmills matched to the way you will actually use one: slow, steady walking for hours, under a standing desk, in a room you also live in.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#walkingpad-z1"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#walking-pad-vs-treadmill" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Walking Pad vs Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#weight-limit" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Weight Limits</a>
          <a href="#belt-and-speed" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Belt and Speed</a>
          <a href="#duty-cycle" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Daily Run Time</a>
          <a href="#desk-setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Desk Setup</a>
          <a href="#noise-floor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Noise and Floors</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Care and Lubrication</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best walking pad is not the one with the highest top speed. It is the one whose weight rating, belt length and daily duty cycle match how many hours you plan to walk at your desk, because that is what decides whether it is still running smoothly a year from now.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specifications, manuals and retailer listings, not from a lab test, and every figure below is attributed to the source that published it. Walking pad specs are unusually inconsistent: the same model can carry a different weight rating on the brand site, Amazon and the printed manual, and motor horsepower is quoted as peak on one page and continuous on another. Where we found that kind of conflict we report both numbers and tell you which one to plan around, which is usually the lower one.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things above everything else. The first is the rated user weight, because running a pad near its limit for hours a day is the fastest way to wear the motor and belt. The second is belt length and width, because a short belt forces a shortened stride and pushes taller walkers toward the front edge. The third is the duty cycle the manufacturer recommends, since some pads are designed for two-hour sessions and others for most of a workday. The fourth is how the pad stores, because most people do not have a spare room for a treadmill.
          </p>

          <p>
            We gave less weight to top speed, app features and remote controls. Almost everyone walking at a desk settles between 1.5 and 3 mph, and a pad that reaches 6 mph is not better for typing at 2 mph. Bluetooth apps were useful on some models and frustrating on others in owner reports, so we treat them as a bonus, not a reason to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we kept the list to machines that are widely sold and supported in the US. Walking pads are a crowded category full of near-identical rebranded units, and spare parts, belt lubricant and warranty service are much easier to get from brands that have been selling the same models for several years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pad-vs-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Walking Pad vs Treadmill
          </h2>

          <p>
            A walking pad is a treadmill with the console, handrails and incline stripped away so it can sit flat under a standing desk. That design makes it lower, lighter and easier to store, but it also limits it. Most walking pads top out between 3.5 and 4 mph, have belts in the 38 to 50 inch range, and use smaller motors that are fine for walking and unhappy with running.
          </p>

          <p>
            A conventional treadmill has a longer, wider belt, a stronger motor and a frame built for impact. It is the better tool for running and for steep incline walking, but it is too tall to sit under a desk and too heavy to slide under a bed. If your goal is to run several times a week, buy a treadmill and keep the desk separate. If your goal is to stop sitting for eight hours a day, a walking pad is the right tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a middle ground. Under-desk treadmills like the LifeSpan TR1200-GlowUp use a full-width, longer deck and a walking-focused motor, and they skip folding entirely. Folding running treadmills like the WalkingPad X25 can reach running speeds and still store small, but they are too tall for most desks with the rail up. Decide first whether you will walk, run or both, and the shortlist gets much shorter.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are building a full home gym, a walking pad pairs well with free weights and a bench, and our <a href="/best-home-gym-equipment-on-a-budget/">budget home gym guide</a> covers the rest of the setup. A good <a href="/best-weight-bench/">weight bench</a> and a walking pad together cover strength and daily movement in very little space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 7 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            These picks are researched from manufacturer specifications, manuals and retailer listings, not lab-tested; every figure is attributed to its source, and where sources disagree we give both numbers. They are ordered by how well they suit all-day desk walking, not by top speed.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
