        <div id="weight-limit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Weight Limits: Plan Around the Lowest Number
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rated user weight is the most important number on a walking pad, and it is the one most often stated inconsistently. Our picks illustrate the problem: the UREVO SpaceWalk 5L is listed at 300, 320 and 400 lb depending on whether you read the EU manual, Amazon or the brand site, and the Egofit M1T is marketed at 220 lb while its manual lists 242 lb. The safe habit is to plan around the lowest published figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even within the rating, headroom matters. A pad rated at 242 lb carrying a 230 lb walker for six hours a day is working near its limit all the time, which loads the motor, heats the belt and wears the deck faster. As a rule of thumb, look for a rating at least 20 to 30 percent above your body weight if you plan to walk for long sessions. Heavier walkers who plan to use a pad all day should look at the 350 lb class, such as the LifeSpan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that the rating covers you plus anything you carry. A heavy backpack or a weighted vest counts. So does the force of a heavier step: walking at a brisk pace puts more load on the deck than strolling, and stepping on and off carelessly can briefly exceed the static rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are close to a pad's limit, check the warranty language too. Several manufacturers exclude damage from exceeding the rated weight, and a motor that fails under overload may not be covered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-and-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Belt Size and Speed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Belt length decides whether you can walk naturally. Most adults walking at 2 to 3 mph take strides somewhere around 26 to 30 inches, and a short belt forces you to shorten that stride or drift toward the front of the deck. The 34 inch surface on the Egofit M1T suits shorter walkers; the roughly 47 to 50 inch belts on the WalkingPad Z1 and LifeSpan suit most adults; anyone over about six feet should favor the longer end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Belt width matters more than people expect when you are typing. When your attention is on a screen, your feet wander side to side. A 15 to 16 inch belt is workable, but a 20 inch belt like the LifeSpan's is noticeably more forgiving over a long day, and it reduces the chance of catching the edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            For speed, ignore the headline maximum. Desk walking almost always happens between 1 and 3 mph, and most people who type while walking settle near 1.5 to 2.5 mph. What matters is that the pad holds a steady speed at the low end and adjusts in small steps. A pad that only changes speed in half-mile increments makes it hard to find the pace where you can still type accurately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running is a different requirement. If you want to jog, you need a model like the WalkingPad X25 that lists running speeds, a longer belt and a stronger motor. Running on a walking-only pad stresses the motor and belt beyond what they were designed for, and many manuals say so explicitly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="duty-cycle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Daily Run Time and Motors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every walking pad has a duty cycle, whether or not it is printed on the box. WalkingPad's listing for the C2, for example, recommends a two-hour maximum session followed by a 15 minute rest so the motor can cool. Under-desk models like the LifeSpan are designed around longer sessions; one review notes its motor is built to support walking for around four hours a day. If you plan to walk most of the workday, that difference matters more than anything else on the spec sheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Motor horsepower figures are hard to compare because brands quote them differently. Some list peak horsepower, which is a brief burst figure; others list continuous horsepower, which is what the motor can sustain. The WalkingPad Z1 shows both 0.75 HP and 2.25 HP on different versions of its own page, and an independent review of a budget Sperax measured far less output than its listing claimed. Treat continuous ratings as the useful number and be skeptical of large peak figures on cheap pads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breaking up long walking sessions helps any pad last longer. Two or three blocks of an hour or two, with the pad off between them, keep the motor and belt cooler than a single eight-hour run. That habit also tends to be easier on your legs and feet when you are new to desk walking.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you notice the belt hesitating under each step, the motor running hot, or the speed dropping as you walk, stop and check the belt tension and lubrication before continuing. Those are early signs the pad is working too hard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="desk-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Setting Up a Walking Desk
          </h2>

          <p>
            A walking pad needs a standing desk that goes high enough. When you stand on a pad, you are typically several inches taller than on the floor, because the deck itself is a few inches tall. Measure your elbow height while standing on the pad and make sure the desk can reach it with your forearms roughly parallel to the floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Position the pad so you walk near the middle of the belt, not the front. Many people push the pad too far under the desk and end up walking on the front third, which shortens the stride and puts the front roller under more load. Leave enough room behind you to step off safely, and keep cables away from the belt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start slow. Most people find they can type comfortably at 1 to 2 mph after a few days and struggle at faster speeds. Phone calls and reading are easier than detailed typing, so many desk walkers walk during meetings and stand still for focused work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shoes matter too. A supportive walking or running shoe is easier on your feet than walking in socks or slippers for hours, and it protects the belt from bare-skin friction. If you also play court sports, our guide to <a href="/best-pickleball-shoes/">court shoes</a> explains why cushioned court shoes are not the same as walking shoes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-floor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Noise, Floors and Shared Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walking pads are quieter than running treadmills, but they are not silent. The motor hums, the belt makes a rhythmic sound under each step, and on hard floors the vibration can travel into the room below. If you live in an apartment or share a home office, the floor underneath matters as much as the pad.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dense rubber mat under the pad absorbs vibration, protects hardwood or vinyl from scratches, and keeps dust from being drawn into the motor housing. The same thick mats sold for home gyms work well; our guides to <a href="/best-garage-floor-mat/">garage floor mats</a> and <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">interlocking garage floor tiles</a> cover rubber options that also suit a basement or garage walking setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carpet is a poor surface for a walking pad. It restricts airflow under the motor, lets the deck flex and pulls fibers into the belt. If carpet is your only option, put a rigid board or a dense mat under the pad.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature affects pads in a garage or basement. Belts and lubricant stiffen in the cold, so a pad stored in an unheated garage may run roughly until it warms up. If your walking setup lives in a cold space, a small <a href="/best-garage-heater/">garage heater</a> or simply bringing the pad inside in winter will help.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Care, Lubrication and Lifespan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most walking pads need two kinds of routine care: keeping the belt centered and tensioned, and lubricating the deck. Manufacturers specify how often to lubricate, commonly every few weeks to every few months depending on use, using the silicone lubricant they supply or recommend. A dry belt drags, makes the motor work harder and wears both belt and deck faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Belt tracking drifts over time, especially if you tend to walk off-center. Most pads have adjustment bolts at the rear roller; small quarter turns, made with the pad running at a slow speed, bring the belt back to center. Check your manual first, because over-tightening can damage the rollers and bearings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the area around and under the pad clean. Dust, pet hair and carpet fibers get drawn into the motor compartment and onto the belt. A quick vacuum around the pad every week or two prevents most of that.
          </p>

          <p>
            With sensible care and use within its rating, a well-built walking pad should last several years of regular use. The quickest ways to shorten that are running it near its weight limit for long sessions, skipping lubrication and ignoring belt slip. If you notice any of those signs, deal with them early; replacement belts and motor controllers are available for the major brands, but they cost a meaningful share of a new pad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Top Speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 6 mph top speed sounds better than 4 mph, but almost nobody types faster than about 2.5 mph. Choose on weight rating, belt length and duty cycle instead, because those decide how long the pad lasts and how comfortable it is under a desk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Highest Weight Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Several walking pads carry different weight ratings on the brand site, retailer listings and manual. Plan around the lowest figure, and leave headroom above your body weight if you will walk for hours a day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Recommended Session Length</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some pads, like the WalkingPad C2, list a two-hour session limit with a cool-down. Walking all day on a pad designed for short sessions overheats the motor and voids some warranties.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Lubrication</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dry belt drags on the deck, makes the motor strain and wears both parts early. Follow the manufacturer's lubrication schedule with the lubricant it specifies.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the Pad on Carpet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carpet blocks airflow to the motor, lets the deck flex and sheds fibers into the belt. Use a dense rubber mat or rigid board on a hard, level floor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

