        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best walking pad for a standing desk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people we recommend the WalkingPad Z1, which WalkingPad lists with a 47.6 by 15.7 inch walking area, a 242 lb rating and a 1 to 4 mph range, and which folds to about 5.4 inches tall. If you plan to walk most of the workday, the LifeSpan TR1200-GlowUp's 20 by 50 inch belt and walking-focused motor are worth the higher price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hours a day can you use a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model. Some, like the WalkingPad C2, carry a recommended two-hour session limit followed by a 15 minute rest. Under-desk models built for long sessions, such as the LifeSpan, are designed to support several hours of walking a day. Check the manual for your model and break long days into blocks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What speed should I walk at while working?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most people can type comfortably between about 1 and 2 mph and handle calls or reading up to about 3 mph. Start slow for the first few days and increase gradually. If your typing accuracy drops or you are drifting on the belt, slow down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are walking pads loud?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are quieter than running treadmills but not silent. You will hear the motor and the rhythm of the belt, and vibration can carry through hard floors into the room below. A dense rubber mat under the pad reduces both noise and vibration.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most walking pads are not designed for running; many top out between 3.5 and 4 mph and use motors sized for walking. If you want to run, choose a model that lists running speeds and a longer belt, such as the WalkingPad X25, which retailers list at 1 to 10 mph.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight can a walking pad hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ratings on our picks range from about 220 lb to 350 lb. Some models list different figures on different pages, so use the lowest published number and leave headroom if you will walk for long sessions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walking pad worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For people who sit most of the day, a walking pad is an easy way to add low-intensity movement without changing their schedule. It is not a replacement for a running treadmill or structured exercise, but walking slowly for an hour or two during work adds up over a week.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

